LOGBOOK.md:1897:- FINDING: mutating `validateCommonRecordEnvelope` to skip its `created_by_host_id` check when the member is ABSENT survives both the new Section 10.1 provenance sweep and the whole existing package suite. Every Section 10.1 validator calls `requireExactMembers` with its closed member list BEFORE `validateIdentityRecordEnvelope`, so an object missing the member is refused as an incomplete member set and the mutated branch is never executed at either identity production entry. Equivalent mutant, not an evidence gap.
LOGBOOK.md-1898-- FINDING, the reachable half, and it was NOT covered: mutating the same check from `requireUUIDv7` to `requireString` also survived the first draft of the sweep, because the sweep's malformed case used an empty string and `requireString` accepts it. A UUIDv4 stamped into a UUIDv7 member is well-formed JSON, well-formed UUID, and names no host — exactly the shape a forged or copied provenance stamp takes.
LOGBOOK.md:1899:- FIX: envelope mutations now include a right-typed / wrong-grammar case per member — UUIDv4 and a non-UUID string for the UUIDv7 members, an impossible calendar date for `created_at`, a non-reverse-DNS key for `extensions`. The mutant dies.
LOGBOOK.md-1900-- DECISION: the absent case is kept but its refusal REASON is pinned to `is missing required member "<member>"`, so it records which gate actually caught it instead of counting as evidence for a clause it never reached. Subsumption named and pinned rather than assumed, per the leaf's front-loaded DoD.
LOGBOOK.md-1901-- NOTE: this is the general shape behind the DoD rule "supply a COMPLETE valid member set and violate exactly one thing". A negative case that deletes a member short-circuits on the member-set gate and proves nothing about the typed gate it is named after.
