LOGBOOK.md-1909-- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "read the spec more carefully": the document uses a CONTROLLED VOCABULARY for array constraints and the implementation treated one of its phrases as a synonym of another. Judgement is the failure mode, so judgement had to leave the loop.
LOGBOOK.md-1910-- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/array_order_inventory_test.go` plus `testdata/array-order-constraints.md`. Ordering sites are derived structurally — a comparison between a value derived from the ranged element and a value bound to an EARLIER element, either carried forward or indexed at an offset from the loop key — so no rename hides one. Strength is read off the comparison OPERATOR (`<=` refuses duplicates, `<` admits them), and the member name is derived by tracing the ranged collection back to the string literal that named it, through helper parameters and across call sites. Order and uniqueness are pinned as SEPARATE factors with their own SPEC lines, because the document frequently declares them at different lines; `sorted unique` must cite a uniqueness declaration and bare `sorted` must cite none. A strengthened validator then cannot be written down consistently.
LOGBOOK.md-1911-- NOTE: the gate proves its own coverage three ways rather than one. A site whose member cannot be traced is REPORTED, which is what a new ordering helper with no call site looks like — an unpinned rule that contributes no row and would otherwise slip past a sequence comparison entirely. Every production refusal whose message speaks about order must belong to a function carrying a derived site, which catches a check written in a shape the tracer does not model. And the mapping is a pure function with its own negative proof.
LOGBOOK.md:1912:- NOTE, a survivor worth recording: the first version of that negative proof asserted only "some problem was reported". Narrowing the missing-uniqueness-citation clause SURVIVED, because the neighbouring default branch reported a different problem for the same row. Each case now pins the exact message it must produce, and a further mutant confirmed the matcher itself is exact. An isolated case must pin its own clause, not the fact that something complained.
LOGBOOK.md-1913-- DISCLOSURE, not repaired here: `WorkspaceSnapshot.members` and `GitIndex.entries` enforce uniqueness the pinned document declares nowhere — an equal `workspace_id` and an equal `(path, stage)` pair respectively. Both sit in `closed_shapes.go`, in sections owned by `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`, inside a reviewed and accepted candidate. They are recorded as disclosed strengthenings with their exact undeclared refusal and routed, and the disclosed set is asserted exactly against the artifact in both directions so a fourth cannot appear silently.
LOGBOOK.md:1914:- EVIDENCE: 26 mutants, 26 killed, 0 survivors, guarded baseline green before and after. Twelve flip an ordering operator at every derived site in the package (six strengthenings, six widenings), five attack the provenance checker itself, five edit the pinned artifact, and two add a second ordering helper and an order refusal in an unmodelled shape as new production files.
