LOGBOOK.md-1921-- FINDING: two live gates in `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go` were proven in one direction only, and review reproduced both end to end against the whole configured gate set. Narrowing `requiresError != errorPresent` to `requiresError && !errorPresent` made `ValidateObservationEvent` **return nil and attest** a `success` Observation Event carrying `"error_code":"boom"`. Narrowing `epoch != 1` to `epoch >= 3` made `CalculateObjectIdentity` **attest** an epoch-2 Lease Record with a null `predecessor_lease_id`. `go test ./...`, `tracecheck` and all four seeded fuzz targets stayed green through both.
LOGBOOK.md:1922:- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "we missed a case": the previous three sweeps rewrote comparison OPERATORS and deleted guards. Neither operation can express either shape. A boolean-valued `!=` has no operator worth rewriting — the mutant is a split of the identity into its two single-sided halves. And `epoch != 1` narrowed to `epoch > 1` is an EQUIVALENT mutant in a positive-integer domain, so an operator sweep reports a kill and says nothing; only a mutant carrying a different LITERAL (`>= K+2`, `!= K+1`) can move the boundary. A sweep proves exactly as much as its grammar can say.
LOGBOOK.md-1923-- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/coupling_derivation_test.go` derives both subjects from the production sources. Boolean-valued is decided from the source — a negation, a nested comparison, a short-circuit, or an identifier the enclosing function binds from a package function whose declared result at that position is `bool` — so the coupling set is not a list. Integer-literal comparisons need no type inference at all: Go refuses to compile a comparison between an untyped integer constant and a non-numeric operand, so the compiler has already established the other side. `coupling_proofs_test.go` obliges two single-sided proofs per coupling (the pair chosen by the operator) and one proof at each value where a literal comparison flips (read off the operator), and asserts obligations equal proofs in both directions.
LOGBOOK.md-1924-- NOTE: the derived key carries the literal, so moving a literal fails the gate BEFORE any case runs. Moving `epoch != 1` to `epoch != 5` was verified to redden the coverage assertion rather than merely one case.
LOGBOOK.md-1925-- DECISION: the Observation result/error and tombstone couplings are driven for EVERY value the production vocabulary admits, resolved through the same derivation the closed-vocabulary inventory uses, rather than for one hand-picked value. Admit by derivation, refuse the complement.
LOGBOOK.md-1926-- DECISION: three boundary values are unreachable and declared, not dropped — a zero `sequence` and a zero `epoch` are refused by the positive-integer gate first. Each names its subsuming refusal and a test that must exist. The one function whose comparisons run at package initialization over the pinned catalog is exempted by name, and the exemption asserts from the sources that every caller of it is a package-level initializer that panics.
LOGBOOK.md:1927:- EVIDENCE: 40 mutants generated from that same derivation — both single-direction narrowings of every derived coupling, and literal shifts an operator grammar cannot express — 40 killed, 0 survivors, green baseline before and after. Five self-checks confirm the gates can fail: a deleted coupling proof, a deleted boundary proof, a duplicated claim, a moved production literal, and a narrowed derivation each redden the suite.
LOGBOOK.md-1928-- SCOPE, stated rather than implied: the literal-boundary gate covers `core_records.go`, this leaf's file. `canonical.go` and `closed_shapes.go` remain owned by the sibling `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`. The presence-coupling gate is package-wide, because eight sites is small enough that scoping it would have left the two `validateGitFeatures` couplings — a known instance of the same class — unproven.
