LOGBOOK.md-1961-- FINDING: the 47-row closed-vocabulary pin from the previous entry works, and it is still not the admitted set. The admitted set is `member list INTERSECT matcher semantics`, and the pin covers only the first factor. Case-folding the comparison inside `requireEnum` made `CalculateObjectIdentity` **accept and attest** a Lease Record whose `reason` was `CREATE`; trimming made it attest `" create "`; relaxing `requireExactString` from equality to a prefix test made it attest a Checkpoint Record whose `status` was `validated_but_not_really`. `go test ./...`, all four seeded fuzz corpora, `tracecheck`, the 47-row pin AND the 97.1% refusal-coverage gate stayed green through every one of them, because the pinned rows still match the source and the refusal branch is still executed for whichever outside value a test happened to pick.
LOGBOOK.md-1962-- ROOT CAUSE, one level deeper than the last three entries: an inventory pins the ARGUMENTS a gate is given. Nothing pinned the comparison. `requireEnum` is the single admission primitive behind all 47 pinned call sites and `requireExactString` behind 32 more that no inventory covered at all, so one edit to either moved 74 call sites at once, invisibly.
LOGBOOK.md:1963:- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/string_admission_test.go`. Deciders are derived from the sources — a function that reads a named member out of a candidate object and decides its admission from a string parameter of its own — deliberately broad about HOW it compares, so `EqualFold` or `HasPrefix` keeps a decider inside its own gate instead of making it vanish. Forwarders are not pinned: the walk visits the inner call and resolves member and values in the SAME frame chain out through the forwarder's own callers, so a wrapper contributes one site per concrete caller rather than a Cartesian product. Sites bind to fixture positions by BEHAVIOUR — a site binds only where every declared member is admitted — which both disambiguates two `kind` vocabularies and makes a NARROWED matcher fail, since a matcher that drops a member binds nowhere. Each bound position is then attacked at both identity entries with a family built from the admitted value itself: three case folds, four whitespace forms, a proper prefix, a proper suffix, two proper superstrings, an embedded NUL. 11/11 matcher mutants killed.
LOGBOOK.md-1964-- FINDING, same shape for grammars: widening `observationNamePattern` from `{1,7}` to `{1,8}` segments, to `{0,7}` so a bare single-segment name is admitted, and adding a hyphen to its character class each survived the whole gate set, as did `boardLogicalIDPattern` at `{0,127}` → `{0,128}`.
LOGBOOK.md:1965:- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/grammar_inventory_test.go` derives every `regexp.MustCompile` in the package with its POLARITY derived from use, pins each against a reference written independently of the source, derives one obligation per anchor, character class, counted bound and one-or-more quantifier FROM THE REFERENCE, and requires a witness that the reference refuses and that the reference with exactly that dimension widened admits — so a witness that could not fail against a widened pattern is rejected before it is trusted. 71 derived mutants + 4 reviewer-named + 3 controls, all killed.
LOGBOOK.md:1966:- NOTE, the sharpest lesson of the round: **a single witness per character class proves nothing about a narrower widening.** A control mutant widening `providerIdentityKeyPattern` to admit uppercase SURVIVED the witness table, because the mechanical widening the table verifies against is maximal (`any character`) while the mutant added only `A-Z`. The fix is not more witnesses: it is a corpus. Every one-character neighbour of the value the fixture already carries — each position substituted by each of twelve character families, each family inserted at each boundary, each position deleted — is driven through the production entry. Nothing about it is hand-written, and the template is derived by probing rather than wired.
LOGBOOK.md-1967-- FINDING, invented-constraint class #4, caught by a gate this time: three grammars carried a "pinned SPEC declaration" for a document that states the rule only in prose. `TestEveryPinnedGrammarReferenceQuotesItsSpecDeclaration` requires the reference to appear verbatim in its own quoted declaration or to disclose the composition, and named `lowerSnakePattern`, `mediaTypePattern` and `windowsAbsolutePathPattern`. All three are now recorded as implementation-defined with the prose quoted rather than cited.
LOGBOOK.md-1968-- FINDING: `opaque_identity` was an empty map in the Provider Identity fixture, so `providerIdentityKeyPattern` was reachable from NO fixture and proven by no derived sweep. An empty open map is a silent hole in every fixture-walking gate. The fixture now carries one member, and `closedObjectMemberPaths` learned the open-map set (`extensions`, `env_literals`, `opaque_identity`) with the clause for each quoted, since their keys are data rather than schema members.
LOGBOOK.md:1969:- DECISION: gates that cannot fail when weakened were treated as defects, not as style. Two of eleven self-attack mutants survived their first run — deleting a dimension from the adversarial family, and disabling the vocabulary provenance check — and both forced a fix: the family has a pinned dimension list, the provenance check is a pure function driven against rows wrong in each way it claims to detect, and the neighbour generator's corpus shape is asserted exactly.
LOGBOOK.md-1970-- NOTE: no production Go source changed in this revision. The review had already probed the three survivors and found production CORRECT; what was missing was a gate that fails when the comparison behind them is relaxed. A revision that fixes only tests is the honest response to a verdict about unpinned semantics.
